"See why the Moon goes through phases, measure the length of the month from new moon to new moon, and find out why eclipses happen only in certain seasons of the year.",
"<strong>Phases.</strong> The Sun always lights half of the Moon, the half facing the Sun. As the Moon goes around Earth, we see different amounts of that lit half. At new moon the Moon is between Earth and the Sun, and its lit half faces away from us. At full moon the Moon is on the far side of Earth from the Sun, and we see the whole lit half. Halfway between are the first quarter and third quarter, when we see half of the disk lit. Earth’s shadow has nothing to do with the phases.",
"<strong>The month.</strong> The Moon goes once around Earth against the stars in {#} days. Meanwhile Earth moves along its orbit around the Sun, so the Moon needs about two more days to line up with the Sun again. The time from one new moon to the next is the synodic month, the month of the phases.",
"<strong>Rising and setting.</strong> Earth turns once a day, so the sky turns {#}° per hour. The Sun rises at about {#} am at an equinox. A Moon that is {#}° east of the Sun rises {#} hours after the Sun.",
"<strong>Eclipses.</strong> A solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes in front of the Sun, which can only happen at new moon. A lunar eclipse happens when the Moon passes through Earth’s shadow, which can only happen at full moon. The Moon’s orbit is tilted about {#}° to Earth’s orbit, so at most new and full moons the Moon passes a little above or below the Sun or Earth’s shadow. An eclipse can only happen when a new or full moon comes near one of the nodes, the two places where the Moon crosses the plane of Earth’s orbit. In the demo, a solar eclipse is possible when the Moon is less than {#}° above or below that plane at new moon, and a lunar eclipse when it is less than {#}° from it at full moon.",
